Your readability concern is valid, but I would push back on making primitive bodies choose syntax according to mask storage.

`mask<zero>()` should mean:

> Construct the mask value with every lane inactive.

It should not imply a scalar bitmask. Lowering should decide the representation:

| Mask policy | Lowered false mask |
|---|---|
| AVX-512 predicate | scalar/native predicate `0` |
| Clang comparison vector | vector broadcast of `0` |
| Future Clang boolean vector | boolean-vector broadcast of `false` |
| SVE predicate | appropriate predicate constructor |
| SSE/AVX2/NEON lane mask | zero vector |

Under that contract, a future `clang_v128_pred` using `mask<zero>()` would be entirely natural. The policy renderer would select boolean-vector construction.

The existing SSE/AVX2/NEON implementations using:

```text
call<primitive=set_zero[Vec]>()
```

are the inconsistent part. They work because those extensions happen to define:

```cpp
mask_type == register_type
```

That is not true for the Clang comparison mask:

```cpp
mask_type != register_type  // float and unsigned vectors, for example
```

Using `set_zero<Vec>` for Clang would therefore couple mask construction incorrectly to the data-register type.

I recommend keeping one representation-neutral mask constructor and eventually normalizing the hardware-vector implementations to it:

```text
mask_false → mask<all_false>()
mask_true  → mask<all_true>()
```

These names would be clearer than `mask<zero>()` and `mask<all>()`, which can sound representation-specific. Internally they would dispatch through `MaskPolicy` exactly as today.

So my recommendation is:

- Do not change Clang back to `set_zero`.
- Treat `mask<...>` as semantic construction, not storage syntax.
- Consider renaming `zero`/`all` to `all_false`/`all_true`.
- Later migrate SSE, AVX2, and NEON to the same semantic constructor once their lane-register render rules are present.

That produces the cleanest future behavior: adding `clang_v128_pred` changes only its mask policy and rendering, not every primitive body that constructs a mask.